Audio conversion
-----------------------------

Clipwise converts each recorded track to 16 kHz with FFmpeg 9.0.2
(https://ffmpeg.org/), licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License,
version 2.1 or later, reproduced in full at the end of this file. It is built as
a standalone program, configured without --enable-gpl or --enable-nonfree, with
only the file protocol, the pcm_f32le and wav demuxers, the wav muxer, the
pcm_f32le and pcm_s16le decoders, the pcm_s16le encoder, and the aresample,
aformat, anull and atrim filters (recorder/ffmpeg/build.sh in Clipwise's source).

Source: https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz
SHA-256: 8c3850283eb25fa026482078a04051e0be17347b09ef81a0849bec15a96e002e
A copy of that exact source tarball is available from the same place as the
Clipwise app.

Python
-----------------------------

The capture pipeline runs on Python 3.14.7 from python-build-standalone, release
20260924 (https://github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone), asset
cpython-3.14.7+20260924-aarch64-apple-darwin-install_only_stripped.tar.gz,
SHA-256 f0fb6b233971672dd7add4efa90f8c209a63253e457ffabd0148b9f734638319.
pip, ensurepip and Tcl/Tk are removed from the copy Clipwise carries.

